glibc-gcc: a self-contained x86_64 glibc + gcc toolchain
=========================================================

A Dockerfile that builds a C/C++ toolchain into /opt/glibc-gcc:

- gcc 16.2 (C, C++), binutils 2.47, Linux 6.18 kernel headers,
- glibc from git master with the patches in patches/ applied,

where everything, including the dynamic loader that compiled programs use,
lives under /opt/glibc-gcc. Programs built with it don't touch the system's
/lib, /usr/lib or /usr/include at all, neither at build time nor at run
time. So it is a convenient way to build and run code against a patched or
unreleased glibc without replacing the system one.

The patches are the _dl_find_object fixes and the dl_events_subscribe
prototype from https://github.com/alk/dl-find-object (doc/glibc/). See
https://tinyurl.com/dl-events-subscribe-design for the API design.


Building
--------

    ./fetch-sources.sh          # snapshots all sources into sources/
    docker build -t glibc-gcc .

fetch-sources.sh needs wcurl (from curl) and git. The docker build itself
downloads nothing but the Debian base image and packages. It takes about
10 minutes on 32 cores and needs ~15 GB of free space in the docker storage
while building. The resulting image is ~2.3 GB; /opt/glibc-gcc in it is
~2.1 GB, most of that debug info in glibc's and gcc's libraries.

The image ends with a smoke test (smoke-test.cc): a C++ program with a
thread that calls dl_events_subscribe, built both -static and dynamic and
run. For the dynamic one it checks that the loader and every library come
from /opt/glibc-gcc.


Using it
--------

Inside the image /opt/glibc-gcc/bin is on PATH:

    docker run --rm -it -v $PWD:/src -w /src glibc-gcc
    x86_64-glibc-linux-gnu-g++ -O2 -o hello hello.cc
    ./hello

Tools are prefixed with the target triple: x86_64-glibc-linux-gnu-gcc,
-g++, -ld, -objdump, -readelf, etc. glibc's own programs (ldd, iconv,
locale, localedef, getconf, ...) are in /opt/glibc-gcc/x86_64-glibc-linux-gnu/bin,
ldconfig and friends in .../sbin.

Programs built with it need /opt/glibc-gcc at run time (the loader path is
hardcoded, see below) unless linked with -static.

Existing binaries, built against the system glibc, can also be run on the
patched glibc via its loader, as long as the system glibc is not newer:

    /opt/glibc-gcc/x86_64-glibc-linux-gnu/lib/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2 ./prog

or linked to use it directly with
-Wl,--dynamic-linker=/opt/glibc-gcc/x86_64-glibc-linux-gnu/lib/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2.


Copying the toolchain out of the image
--------------------------------------

The image is built on Debian stable (trixie, glibc 2.41), so the
toolchain's own executables (gcc, cc1plus, as, ld, ...) run on any
reasonably current x86_64 Linux distro: they need nothing from the host but
libc.so.6 and libm.so.6, and currently only use symbols up to GLIBC_2.38.
libstdc++ and gcc's support libraries (gmp, mpfr, mpc, isl) are linked in
statically.

To install it on the host, copy /opt/glibc-gcc to the same path:

    docker create --name glibc-gcc-copy glibc-gcc
    docker cp glibc-gcc-copy:/opt/glibc-gcc - | sudo tar -C /opt -x
    docker rm glibc-gcc-copy
    export PATH=/opt/glibc-gcc/bin:$PATH

docker cp writes a tar stream to stdout here, so only tar needs root, and
it works with a remote docker host too. To take it to another machine,
save that stream to a file instead (docker cp ... - | xz > glibc-gcc.tar.xz)
and unpack it with tar -C /opt -xf there.

It has to end up at /opt/glibc-gcc exactly: compiled programs get
/opt/glibc-gcc/... as their loader, ld.so has its search path compiled in,
and glibc's linker scripts (libc.so, and even libm.a) refer to libraries
by absolute path, so not even -static links work elsewhere. (The gcc
driver itself is relocatable, but that doesn't help much.)
Building it for a different path means changing PREFIX in the Dockerfile.


Build steps
===========

The textbook cross-toolchain recipe builds binutils, a stage 1 (C-only)
gcc, then the libc, then the full gcc, all against a sysroot. For this
particular toolchain (x86_64 host, x86_64 glibc target, fixed install path
/opt/glibc-gcc) most of that isn't needed, so the Dockerfile does less:

- binutils: built (see below why).
- kernel headers, then glibc: glibc is built with Debian's gcc and
  binutils, both are installed straight into gcc's "tooldir"
  (/opt/glibc-gcc/x86_64-glibc-linux-gnu, $TOOLDIR in the Dockerfile).
  No stage 1 gcc.
- gcc: built once, C and C++, no sysroot.

binutils and glibc don't depend on each other, so they are separate Docker
stages that get built in parallel (the kernel headers stage feeds glibc).
The gcc stage copies binutils and glibc in.

Sources come from sources/ (see fetch-sources.sh), not the network. All
versions are ARGs at the top of the Dockerfile; fetch-sources.sh reads them
from there and only fetches what is missing.

gcc's in-tree prerequisites (gmp, mpfr, mpc, isl, gettext) are fetched at
the versions that gcc release pins in contrib/download_prerequisites. In
the docker build, download_prerequisites then finds them already present,
checks their sha512 sums, unpacks them and adds the symlinks, so gcc has no
host library dependencies.


glibc version and patches
-------------------------

The patches are against glibc master (version 2.44.9000;
dl_events_subscribe is a GLIBC_2.45 symbol), not a release, so glibc is the
git snapshot GLIBC_COMMIT. sourceware doesn't serve snapshot tarballs, but
it does allow fetching a single commit, so fetch-sources.sh does a shallow
fetch of that commit and turns it into a tarball with git archive (plus
gzip -n, which gives the same bytes every time).

To move to newer patches: put them into patches/, set GLIBC_COMMIT to the
commit they are based on (git format-patch --base=... records it), and rerun
fetch-sources.sh.


The target triple
-----------------

gcc decides whether it's a cross compiler by comparing the canonical build
and target triples. x86_64-linux-gnu canonicalizes to x86_64-pc-linux-gnu,
which is exactly what config.guess says for the build machine, so gcc would
be built native and search /usr/include, /lib, /usr/lib, ... and Debian's
multiarch dirs. A different vendor field (x86_64-glibc-linux-gnu) changes
nothing about the generated code, but makes it a cross compiler that only
looks in /opt/glibc-gcc (see "No sysroot").

glibc gets the same triple as --host, so its build is a cross build too.
That mostly matters for make install: in a native build it runs ldconfig
and scripts/test-installation.pl, which compiles test programs with $CC
(Debian's gcc, linking against Debian's libc).


Building our own binutils
-------------------------

Not strictly needed. Host and target are both x86_64 ELF, so the assembler
and linker don't care which libc they produce code for; everything
libc-specific (crt files, loader path, search paths) comes from the gcc
driver. gcc could be configured with --with-as=/usr/bin/as
--with-ld=/usr/bin/ld (or symlinks in $TOOLDIR/bin).

Why build it anyway:
- Debian's ld has the multiarch dirs (/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu etc.)
  built in as default search dirs, so a -lfoo that our dirs don't have
  would silently pick up the system one. Ours only searches the tooldir
  (x86_64-glibc-linux-gnu-ld --verbose | grep SEARCH_DIR).
- /opt/glibc-gcc is self-contained and doesn't change behavior when the
  host's binutils is upgraded or copied to another distro.
- gcc's configure probes as/ld features and bakes the results in, so
  binutils should be at least as new as gcc and should not change
  afterwards.
- We get the full x86_64-glibc-linux-gnu-{ar,nm,objcopy,readelf,strip,...}
  set, and a linker we can rebuild or patch for dynamic linker experiments.
- It's cheap (about a minute) and runs in parallel with the glibc build.


No stage 1 gcc
--------------

glibc only needs a C compiler and binutils that produce x86_64 code: it
builds with -nostdlib/-nostdinc where it matters and links libgcc.a from
the compiler it's built with. Debian's gcc and binutils are new enough
(glibc wants gcc >= 12.1, binutils >= 2.39), and glibc's configure deals
with distro hardening defaults (stack protector, PIE, CET, fortify).
gcc then only needs *an* installed glibc (headers, crt files, libc.so) to
build libgcc_s and libstdc++ against, not one built by a stage 1 gcc.
--disable-werror because master plus a gcc newer than what glibc was last
tested with tends to produce new warnings.

Stage 1 is required for real cross targets (aarch64, riscv, ...), which is
why the textbook recipes include it. What we give up is glibc being built
by the same gcc version the toolchain ships. If that ever matters, the next
simplest option is to configure gcc once, `make all-gcc`, build glibc with
the gcc from the build tree, then finish the same gcc build.


No sysroot
----------

Since x86_64-glibc-linux-gnu isn't the build triple, gcc is a cross
compiler, and without a sysroot it never looks at the host's /usr/include,
/lib or /usr/lib. It uses the tooldir ($PREFIX/$TARGET): headers in include
(and sys-include), crt files and libs in lib (and lib/../lib64); our ld
defaults there too. So glibc is configured with --prefix=$TOOLDIR and
installed without DESTDIR, and the kernel headers go to
INSTALL_HDR_PATH=$TOOLDIR.

glibc's libc.so is a linker script with absolute paths
($TOOLDIR/lib/libc.so.6 etc.). Without a sysroot ld uses them as is, which
is right since glibc is installed at its final location.

What a sysroot would buy, if we ever want it:
- A tree shaped like a real root filesystem (usr/include, usr/lib, lib), so
  other libraries can be cross-built with the usual --prefix=/usr
  DESTDIR=$SYSROOT and picked up automatically (also by pkg-config via
  PKG_CONFIG_SYSROOT_DIR). Without one, build them with --prefix=$TOOLDIR.
- ld resolves absolute paths in linker scripts (like libc.so) and -L=
  paths under it.
- It's the modern convention (crosstool-ng, buildroot, Yocto).
- gcc can be moved elsewhere: a sysroot under the prefix is found relative
  to the gcc binary. Not something we need, since the loader path is
  hardcoded to /opt/glibc-gcc anyway.

What it costs: binutils and gcc need --with-sysroot, and gcc also
--with-build-sysroot (the xgcc in the gcc build tree computes the
relocatable sysroot relative to its own location, so building libgcc fails
with "stdio.h: No such file or directory" without it). glibc needs
DESTDIR=$SYSROOT and --prefix=/usr, and its libs end up spread over lib64
and usr/lib64.


sys-include and gcc's limits.h
------------------------------

For a cross gcc without a sysroot, gcc's configure and build look at the
target headers in $TOOLDIR/sys-include (not include):

- configure reads features.h there to learn the glibc version, which turns
  on things like TARGET_LIBC_PROVIDES_SSP and gnu_unique_object;
- LIMITS_H_TEST (gcc/Makefile.in) decides whether gcc's own limits.h
  #include_next's the libc's one. If not, <limits.h> lacks PATH_MAX and
  friends;
- fixincludes processes the headers found there.

So the gcc stage makes $TOOLDIR/sys-include a symlink to include before
building gcc. gcc drops the duplicate directory from the include path (it
compares inodes). The smoke test prints PATH_MAX.

(musl based toolchains get away without this: for *-musl targets gcc's
linux.h puts the libc headers before gcc's own include dir, so musl's
limits.h is found first, and fixincludes is skipped.)


Directory layout
----------------

With a prefix other than /usr, glibc puts both slibdir and rtlddir in
$prefix/lib (only --prefix=/usr gets /lib64 on x86_64). So ld.so is
$TOOLDIR/lib/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2 and libc.so.6 and friends are next to it.
ld.so's built-in search path (used after LD_LIBRARY_PATH, rpath and
ld.so.cache) is that same directory.

gcc installs libstdc++ and libgcc_s into $TOOLDIR/lib/../lib64 (the x86_64
multilib OS dir, even with --disable-multilib). $TOOLDIR/lib64 is a symlink
to lib, so they end up next to libc and ld.so finds them without rpath and
without an ld.so.cache. (The alternative is $TOOLDIR/etc/ld.so.conf listing
lib64, plus rerunning ldconfig after every change to the dirs.) Our ld
searches $TOOLDIR/lib64 and $TOOLDIR/lib by default, which is now the same
directory twice; harmless.

glibc's make install also puts its programs into $TOOLDIR/bin, next to
binutils' unprefixed as/ld/... (which gcc runs), and ldconfig, zic etc.
into $TOOLDIR/sbin. None of the names clash. ldd is a script that runs our
ld.so on the binary.


Dynamic loader at /opt/glibc-gcc
--------------------------------

- gcc, not glibc, hardcodes the loader path (PT_INTERP) written into
  executables: GLIBC_DYNAMIC_LINKER64 in gcc/config/i386/linux64.h. The
  Dockerfile seds it to $TOOLDIR/lib/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2.
- ld.so finds libs in its own directory (see "Directory layout").
- $TOOLDIR/etc/ld.so.cache doesn't exist (cross builds don't run
  ldconfig). If you add $TOOLDIR/etc/ld.so.conf, run $TOOLDIR/sbin/ldconfig
  after changes.
- The tooldir only matters at compile and link time. At run time only
  absolute paths count.


Runtime files from the host
---------------------------

glibc derives some runtime paths from the configured prefix and hardcodes
others:

- Under $TOOLDIR: ld.so.cache, gconv modules (lib/gconv), compiled locales
  (lib/locale, empty: C and C.UTF-8 are built in; build others with our
  localedef, or point LOCPATH elsewhere).
- Host /etc, hardcoded: nsswitch.conf, resolv.conf, hosts, gai.conf, and
  ld.so.preload (so the host's preload file, if any, applies). NSS modules
  named in the host's nsswitch.conf that we don't have (systemd, mdns,
  ...) are skipped.
- Time zones: glibc ships no tzdata, and by default would look for it in
  $TOOLDIR/share/zoneinfo and for the local zone in $TOOLDIR/etc/localtime,
  so everything would be UTC. The glibc stage's configparms points zonedir
  and localtime-file at /usr/share/zoneinfo and /etc/localtime instead.


gcc configure flags
-------------------

--disable-bootstrap: the host gcc is recent, and a 3-stage bootstrap would
triple the build time for nothing we need. --disable-multilib: no 32-bit
or x32 runtimes. libsanitizer stays enabled (-fsanitize=address etc. work).

The gcc stage deletes its source and build trees in the same RUN as make
install. Otherwise that layer is ~8 GB.
